    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>For skin, marine collagen is the slightly better pick, and the margin is smaller
        than brands claim.</strong> Marine is close to pure type 1, the collagen that makes up most
        of your dermis. A 2024 crossover trial found fish and bovine peptides absorb comparably, so
        the real deciders are dietary fit, taste and cost. Bovine adds type 3 and costs less, but it
        is cattle hide.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Source:</strong> marine collagen comes from fish skin and scales, usually cod, tilapia or snapper. Bovine collagen comes from cow hide and bone.</li>
        <li><strong>Types:</strong> marine is 90% or more type 1. Bovine is a type 1 and type 3 mix, which is why it gets sold for gut and connective tissue as much as for skin.</li>
        <li><strong>Dietary fit:</strong> beef-derived bovine collagen is a hard no for a large share of Indian buyers. Marine sidesteps that, though neither one is vegetarian.</li>
        <li><strong>Absorption:</strong> a 2024 randomised crossover trial compared fish, porcine and bovine peptides in the same people and concluded absorption is "highly comparable". Fish led on one signalling fragment; it is not the large advantage marine marketing describes.</li>
        <li><strong>Honest limitation:</strong> no adequately powered trial has put marine against bovine on the same skin measurements in the same people. A placebo-controlled head-to-head is registered but has published no results, so the skin comparison is still unsettled.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

