      <h2 id="absorption">Is marine collagen absorbed better than bovine collagen?</h2>

      <p>
        Largely no, and this is the part of the article I have had to rewrite. When
        somebody finally ran the comparison properly in humans, the gap mostly disappeared. Virgilio
        and colleagues published a randomised, double-blind crossover trial in
        <em>Frontiers in Nutrition</em> in 2024 that gave the same volunteers fish, porcine and
        bovine collagen hydrolysate, the bovine at both 2,000 and 5,000 Daltons, and measured what
        reached their blood. Their conclusion was that absorption of the three sources is "highly
        comparable".
      </p>

      <p>
        Here is why size decides the outcome. Iwai and colleagues showed in 2005 that specific small
        fragments, Pro-Hyp and Hyp-Gly, survive digestion intact and appear circulating in human
        blood after a dose of collagen hydrolysate. Those fragments seem to act as signals rather
        than as bricks, reaching the dermis and prompting fibroblasts, your collagen-making cells,
        to increase output. Peptides below roughly 2,000 Daltons cross the gut wall efficiently.
        Above about 5,000, much less does.
      </p>

      <p>
        So here are the actual numbers. Free hydroxyproline in plasma rose by an average factor of
        about 7.2 after fish collagen, 9.9 after porcine and 6.2 after both bovine preparations:
        porcine, not marine, came out highest on that measure. Where fish did lead was Hyp-Gly, one
        of the signalling fragments, at 2.3 times the level seen after low-molecular-weight bovine
        (p = 0.0050). Porcine beat the same bovine preparation on two fragments as well. Read
        honestly, that is a source-specific difference in which peptides appear, not a wholesale
        absorption advantage for marine.
      </p>

      <p>
        One detail in that trial deserves its own sentence, because it quietly dismantles a
        marketing claim. Bovine hydrolysed to 2,000 Daltons and bovine left at 5,000 Daltons
        produced the <em>same</em> rise in free hydroxyproline. Molecular weight is the entire basis
        of the "smaller peptides absorb better" pitch, and in this trial moving it did not move the
        outcome.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two caveats, in both directions. The 2024 trial measured a single dose and what appeared in
        blood over the following hours, not skin twelve weeks later, so it cannot tell you which
        source makes a better supplement. And the trial that could, where one group takes marine,
        another takes bovine and somebody measures their skin at twelve weeks, <strong>still does
        not exist at any useful size.</strong> A placebo-controlled comparison of bovine and marine
        collagen is registered on ClinicalTrials.gov as NCT06603194 and has published no results.
        Until it does, anyone telling you marine is clinically proven superior for skin is
        extrapolating. I am keeping the earlier figure of about 1.5 times out of this article
        because the human trial does not support it.
      </p>

