      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Is bovine collagen made from beef?</summary>
          <p>
            Yes. Bovine collagen is extracted from cattle hide, bone and connective tissue, which
            are byproducts of the beef industry. A grass fed label describes how the cattle were
            raised and does not change the source. If beef is not acceptable to you, marine collagen
            from fish is the usual alternative.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which is better for skin, marine or bovine collagen?</summary>
          <p>
            Marine, by a narrow margin, and not for the reason most brands give. It is over 90% type
            1, the dominant collagen in the dermis. The absorption argument has weakened: a 2024
            randomised crossover trial gave the same people fish, porcine and bovine peptides and
            concluded absorption is comparable. With no powered trial comparing skin outcomes, your
            dose, your vitamin C and whether you take it daily matter more than the animal.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is fish collagen the same as marine collagen?</summary>
          <p>
            Yes, the terms are used interchangeably. Both refer to collagen extracted from fish skin
            and scales, most commonly cod, tilapia or snapper. Some marine collagen comes from
            jellyfish or sponges instead, but fish skin accounts for almost everything sold in India.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is marine or bovine collagen suitable for vegetarians?</summary>
          <p>
            Neither one is. Marine collagen is fish-derived and bovine collagen is cattle-derived,
            so both are non-vegetarian. Products sold as vegan collagen contain no collagen at all,
            only amino acids and co-factors such as vitamin C intended to support your own
            production, and the evidence behind them is considerably weaker.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does marine collagen taste fishy?</summary>
          <p>
            It can, and that depends on processing rather than on marine collagen as a category.
            Poorly deodorised powder carries a distinct fishy note, while well-processed marine
            collagen is close to tasteless. Flavoured ready-to-drink shots mask it completely, which
            matters because daily consistency for twelve weeks is what produces results.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I take marine and bovine collagen together?</summary>
          <p>
            You can, and there is no safety issue, but there is little reason to. Both supply type 1
            collagen and it is the total daily dose that matters, with published trials using 2.5g
            to 10g. Spending on one well-dosed source makes more sense than splitting your money
            across two under-dosed ones.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Has anyone actually compared marine and bovine collagen head to head?</summary>
          <p>
            For absorption, yes, once. Virgilio and colleagues gave the same volunteers fish,
            porcine and bovine hydrolysate in a 2024 randomised crossover trial and concluded the
            three absorb comparably. For skin, hair or nail outcomes, no adequately powered trial
            has done it: a placebo-controlled comparison of bovine and marine collagen is registered
            as NCT06603194 and has published no results. Until it reports, any claim that one source
            beats the other on skin is extrapolation.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does marine collagen contain mercury or heavy metals?</summary>
          <p>
            It is a fair question, because fish do accumulate mercury. A 2025 analysis in
            <em>Open Medicine</em> tested marine collagen supplements for lead, cadmium, chromium,
            mercury and arsenic, and the jellyfish and mackerel-skin samples it examined showed no
            detectable toxic metals. The same authors were careful to note how few data exist on
            toxic metals in marine collagen, which limits how much reassurance anyone should draw.
            Collagen is extracted from skin and scales rather than the muscle and fat where
            methylmercury concentrates, which is a reasonable argument rather than a tested one, so
            the practical step is to ask a brand for a batch certificate of analysis.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is marine or bovine collagen halal?</summary>
          <p>
            Marine collagen is generally treated as permissible, since fish do not require ritual
            slaughter. Bovine collagen is treated as doubtful unless it carries certification
            covering the slaughter, so an uncertified bovine tub is the harder case of the two.
            Check any capsule shell or gummy base separately from the collagen, because those are
            often a different animal again. These are the general positions taken in halal
            certification guidance rather than a ruling, so confirm with your own authority if it
            matters to you.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

